window.docContents[213].resolve({"/2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory/2___10___-Historical-interlude___-Sophus-Lie___s-trials-and-transformations/Historical-interlude-on-Sophus-Lie-_LPAR_continued-and-conclusion_RPAR_/#Chapter2___Discussion_2___10_continued":{"contents":"and preferred citing his own earlier papers [47, p. 310]. He had even less luck with the choice of assistant to write up results on contact transformations and partial differential equations. Felix Hausdorff's interests led him elsewhere, and Lie's thoughts on these subjects were never completely spelled out [16, p. 324]. Thus we may never discover the \"true Lie\".\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00092. Basic notions of representation theory\u00092.10. Historical interlude: Sophus Lie's trials and transformations\u0009Historical interlude on Sophus Lie (continued and conclusion)","header":"Historical interlude on Sophus Lie (continued and conclusion)","id":"/2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory/2___10___-Historical-interlude___-Sophus-Lie___s-trials-and-transformations/Historical-interlude-on-Sophus-Lie-_LPAR_continued-and-conclusion_RPAR_/#Chapter2___Discussion_2___10_continued"},"/2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory/2___3___-Representations/Schur___s-lemma-for-algebraically-closed-fields/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory--2___3___-Representations--Schur___s-lemma-for-algebraically-closed-fields":{"contents":"\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00092. Basic notions of representation theory\u00092.3. Representations","header":"Schur's lemma for algebraically closed fields","id":"/2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory/2___3___-Representations/Schur___s-lemma-for-algebraically-closed-fields/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory--2___3___-Representations--Schur___s-lemma-for-algebraically-closed-fields"},"/3___-General-results-of-representation-theory/3___1___-Subrepresentations-in-semisimple-representations/Generalization-of-Proposition-3___1___4-to-non-algebraically-closed-fields/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--3___-General-results-of-representation-theory--3___1___-Subrepresentations-in-semisimple-representations--Generalization-of-Proposition-3___1___4-to-non-algebraically-closed-fields":{"contents":"\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00093. General results of representation theory\u00093.1. Subrepresentations in semisimple representations","header":"Generalization of Proposition 3.1.4 to non-algebraically-closed fields","id":"/3___-General-results-of-representation-theory/3___1___-Subrepresentations-in-semisimple-representations/Generalization-of-Proposition-3___1___4-to-non-algebraically-closed-fields/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--3___-General-results-of-representation-theory--3___1___-Subrepresentations-in-semisimple-representations--Generalization-of-Proposition-3___1___4-to-non-algebraically-closed-fields"},"/3___-General-results-of-representation-theory/3___5___-Finite-dimensional-algebras/Radical-of-a-finite-dimensional-algebra/#Chapter3___Definition3___5___1___formalization":{"contents":"\n\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00093. General results of representation theory\u00093.5. Finite dimensional algebras\u0009Radical of a finite dimensional algebra\u0009Radical of a finite dimensional algebra","header":"Formalization","id":"/3___-General-results-of-representation-theory/3___5___-Finite-dimensional-algebras/Radical-of-a-finite-dimensional-algebra/#Chapter3___Definition3___5___1___formalization"},"/3___-General-results-of-representation-theory/3___8___-The-Krull-Schmidt-theorem/Krull-Schmidt-for-modules-of-finite-length/#Chapter3___Remark3___8___6":{"contents":"Remark 3.8.6. Thus, we see that, in general, the Krull-Schmidt theorem fails for infinite dimensional modules. However, it still holds for modules of finite length, i.e., modules M such that any filtration of M has length bounded above by a certain constant l = l(M).\n\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00093. General results of representation theory\u00093.8. The Krull-Schmidt theorem\u0009Krull-Schmidt for modules of finite length","header":"Krull-Schmidt for modules of finite length","id":"/3___-General-results-of-representation-theory/3___8___-The-Krull-Schmidt-theorem/Krull-Schmidt-for-modules-of-finite-length/#Chapter3___Remark3___8___6"},"/4___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Basic-results/4___2___-Characters/Representations-determined-by-characters-in-characteristic-0/#Chapter4___Corollary4___2___4___formalization":{"contents":"\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00094. Representations of finite groups: Basic results\u00094.2. Characters\u0009Representations determined by characters in characteristic 0\u0009Representations determined by characters in characteristic 0","header":"Formalization","id":"/4___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Basic-results/4___2___-Characters/Representations-determined-by-characters-in-characteristic-0/#Chapter4___Corollary4___2___4___formalization"},"/6___-Quiver-representations/6___2___-Indecomposable-representations-of-the-quivers-___A_1______-___A_2______-___A_3___/Example-6___2___2___-Indecomposable-representations-of-A1/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--6___-Quiver-representations--6___2___-Indecomposable-representations-of-the-quivers-___A_1______-___A_2______-___A_3___--Example-6___2___2___-Indecomposable-representations-of-A1":{"contents":"\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00096. Quiver representations\u00096.2. Indecomposable representations of the quivers $A_1$, $A_2$, $A_3$","header":"Example 6.2.2: Indecomposable representations of A1","id":"/6___-Quiver-representations/6___2___-Indecomposable-representations-of-the-quivers-___A_1______-___A_2______-___A_3___/Example-6___2___2___-Indecomposable-representations-of-A1/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--6___-Quiver-representations--6___2___-Indecomposable-representations-of-the-quivers-___A_1______-___A_2______-___A_3___--Example-6___2___2___-Indecomposable-representations-of-A1"},"/7___-Introduction-to-categories/7___1___-The-definition-of-a-category/Section-7___1___-The-definition-of-a-category/#Chapter7___Introduction_7___1___heading-1":{"contents":"We have now seen many examples of representation theories and of operations with representations (direct sum, tensor product, induction, restriction, reflection functors, etc.). A context in which one can systematically talk about this is provided by category theory.\n\nCategory theory was founded by Saunders Mac Lane and Samuel Eilenberg in 1942—1945. It is a fairly abstract theory which seemingly has no content, for which reason it was christened \"abstract nonsense\" (see Section 7.10). Nevertheless, it is a very flexible and powerful language, which has become totally indispensable in many areas of mathematics, such as algebraic geometry, topology, representation theory, and many others.\n\nWe will now give a very short introduction to category theory, highlighting its relevance to the topics in representation theory we have discussed. For a serious acquaintance with category theory, the reader may use, for instance, the classical book [McL].\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00097. Introduction to categories\u00097.1. The definition of a category\u0009Section 7.1: The definition of a category\u0009Section 7.1: The definition of a category","header":"7.1. The definition of a category","id":"/7___-Introduction-to-categories/7___1___-The-definition-of-a-category/Section-7___1___-The-definition-of-a-category/#Chapter7___Introduction_7___1___heading-1"},"/7___-Introduction-to-categories/7___3___-Morphisms-of-functors/Natural-transformation/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--7___-Introduction-to-categories--7___3___-Morphisms-of-functors--Natural-transformation":{"contents":"\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00097. Introduction to categories\u00097.3. Morphisms of functors","header":"Natural transformation","id":"/7___-Introduction-to-categories/7___3___-Morphisms-of-functors/Natural-transformation/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--7___-Introduction-to-categories--7___3___-Morphisms-of-functors--Natural-transformation"},"/7___-Introduction-to-categories/7___5___-Representable-functors/Yoneda-Lemma/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--7___-Introduction-to-categories--7___5___-Representable-functors--Yoneda-Lemma--Yoneda-Lemma--Formalization--Primary-declarations":{"contents":"Every isomorphism between representable Yoneda functors is induced by a unique isomorphism of\nthe represented objects.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter7/Lemma7.5.1; role=primary]\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00097. Introduction to categories\u00097.5. Representable functors\u0009Yoneda Lemma\u0009Yoneda Lemma\u0009Formalization","header":"Primary declarations","id":"/7___-Introduction-to-categories/7___5___-Representable-functors/Yoneda-Lemma/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--7___-Introduction-to-categories--7___5___-Representable-functors--Yoneda-Lemma--Yoneda-Lemma--Formalization--Primary-declarations"},"/8___-Homological-algebra/8___2___-Tor-and-Ext-functors/Tor-and-Ext-for-tensor-products-of-algebras/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--8___-Homological-algebra--8___2___-Tor-and-Ext-functors--Tor-and-Ext-for-tensor-products-of-algebras--Tor-and-Ext-for-tensor-products-of-algebras--Formalization--Supporting-declarations":{"contents":"Under finite projective resolution hypotheses and compatibility of scalar multiplication on pure tensors, asserts the existence of a degreewise isomorphism with the indexed sum of tensor products in complementary degrees.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter8/Problem8.2.8; role=supporting]\n\nFor opposite-side module objects and a componentwise scalar action on pure tensors, asserts the existence of a degreewise isomorphism with the indexed sum over complementary degrees.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter8/Problem8.2.8; role=supporting]\n\nUnder componentwise scalar-action hypotheses, asserts the existence of a linear equivalence between the degree-indexed tensor-product object and the direct sum of tensor products indexed by pairs whose degrees add to the chosen index.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter8/Problem8.2.8; role=supporting]\n\nThe canonical distribution map from a tensor product of duals of finitely supported sequences is not surjective.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter8/Problem8.2.8/Derived2; role=supporting]\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00098. Homological algebra\u00098.2. Tor and Ext functors\u0009Tor and Ext for tensor products of algebras\u0009Tor and Ext for tensor products of algebras\u0009Formalization","header":"Supporting declarations","id":"/8___-Homological-algebra/8___2___-Tor-and-Ext-functors/Tor-and-Ext-for-tensor-products-of-algebras/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--8___-Homological-algebra--8___2___-Tor-and-Ext-functors--Tor-and-Ext-for-tensor-products-of-algebras--Tor-and-Ext-for-tensor-products-of-algebras--Formalization--Supporting-declarations"}});